            "disagreement": "Both assessments reach the same verdict (support, high severity, useful), so there is no substantive conflict on the outcome. They differ only in rigor of justification: one supplies an explicit witness input, a truth-table argument tying the observed outputs to the frozen source body, and an explicit caveat that the negation arm exercises an in-probe reference policy rather than a mutated copy of the shipped source (so the executed pair proves harness sensitivity, and only the baseline is source-bound). The other offers no counterexample or control analysis at all, asserting validity without reasoning. I preserve the first assessment's caveat and note that the executed-evidence packet itself reports execution_classification \"unproven\" and controller_source_binding false; I therefore do not rest the verdict on the executed differential, and treat the run outputs only as corroboration of a conclusion that the frozen source text establishes on its own.",
